      "blocks": {
        "expert": {
          "type": "expert",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/adv-board-mackenzie.webp",
            "name": "DR RICHARD MACKENZIE · LEADING METABOLISM RESEARCHER, PUBLISHED AUTHOR.",
            "quote": "“Your body doesn’t read the label. It digests the protein, pools the amino acids, and spends them on whatever it decides is most urgent.”",
            "note": "<p>Skin is rarely top of that list. And what isn’t spent doesn’t sit in a queue waiting - it gets burned or stored.</p>",
            "signature": "",
            "image_position": "left",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "head_myth": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "01 · The myth",
            "title": "Eating a protein does not deliver that protein to where you want it.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_myth": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The implied promise of a collagen supplement is a supply chain: drink collagen, arrive as collagen, end up in skin. That isn’t how protein works. Collagen is far too large to cross the gut wall intact. Stomach acid and digestive enzymes break it into peptides, and most of those into individual amino acids, which enter the bloodstream and join the same free amino acid pool as the protein in your dinner.</p><p>From there the body allocates. It builds what it currently needs - enzymes, immune proteins, muscle, gut lining, skin - according to demand, not according to what the tub said. Glycine and proline are useful raw materials for collagen synthesis, but supplying more of them does not instruct a fibroblast to build more collagen, any more than delivering more bricks instructs a builder to start work.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "myth1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "What the label implies",
            "title": "Collagen in, collagen out",
            "text": "<p>A direct delivery from the scoop to the dermis, as though the molecule kept its identity all the way through.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "myth2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "What digestion does",
            "title": "Breaks it into parts",
            "text": "<p>Peptides and free amino acids enter circulation. The original structure is gone before absorption.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "myth3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "What decides the outcome",
            "title": "The cell, not the supplement",
            "text": "<p>Synthesis happens where there is demand and the energy to run it. That is a capacity question.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "head_surplus": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "02 · The surplus",
            "title": "Amino acids you don’t use become glucose or fat.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_surplus": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is the part the category rarely mentions. Unlike fat and carbohydrate, the body has no store for surplus amino acids. There is no protein reserve to draw on later. Anything above immediate demand is deaminated in the liver: the nitrogen is stripped off and excreted as urea, and the carbon skeleton left behind is fed into metabolism.</p><p>Where it goes from there is standard biochemistry. Glucogenic amino acids - glycine, proline and alanine among them, which is most of what collagen is made of - are converted through pyruvate into glucose or stored as glycogen. Ketogenic amino acids feed into acetyl-CoA, and in a calorie surplus that pathway ends in fat. Either way the amino acid stops being a building material and becomes fuel or storage.</p><p>So a daily scoop of collagen taken on top of an already adequate protein intake is not banked for your skin. It is oxidised, converted, or stored, and the wrinkle it was bought for is unaffected.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "step1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 01",
            "title": "Digested",
            "text": "<p>Broken into peptides and free amino acids in the gut.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "step2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 02",
            "title": "Pooled",
            "text": "<p>Mixed into one circulating supply with every other protein you ate.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "step3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 03",
            "title": "Allocated",
            "text": "<p>Spent on whatever the body currently judges most urgent.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "step4": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "4",
            "label": "Step 04",
            "title": "Burned or stored",
            "text": "<p>The surplus is deaminated. Carbon goes to glucose or fat; nitrogen leaves as urea.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "statement": {
          "type": "statement",
          "settings": {
            "text": "There is no protein store. Anything you don’t have a use for today is fuel by tomorrow."
          }
        },
        "head_constraint": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "03 · The real constraint",
            "title": "Skin isn’t short of materials. It’s short of capacity.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_constraint": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>If you eat enough protein, you are not short of glycine or proline. What changes with age is the cell’s ability to do the work: fibroblasts turning over collagen, repair enzymes clearing damage, mitochondria funding all of it. Every one of those processes runs on energy, and skin is metabolically expensive tissue that renews constantly.</p><p>NAD⁺ is the coenzyme that funds it. Mitochondria use it to turn food into energy; repair enzymes need it to work at all. It declines steadily with age, and when supply is short the body prioritises - energy production is non-negotiable, structural maintenance is deferrable. Skin sits firmly in the deferrable column.</p><p>Which reframes the question. Adding more raw material to a system that is short of energy doesn’t produce more collagen. It produces more surplus.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "constraint1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "What collagen supplements address",
            "title": "The supply of raw material",
            "text": "<p>Rarely the limiting factor in anyone eating adequate protein.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "constraint2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "What they don’t address",
            "title": "The energy to build with it",
            "text": "<p>Cellular energy and repair capacity, both of which fall with age.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "head_why": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "04 · Why he worked with MUTO",
            "title": "Work on the capacity, not the raw material.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_why": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>MUTO doesn’t supply building blocks. It works on the layer beneath - producing NAD⁺, protecting it from being broken down as fast as it is made, and giving the cell what it needs to spend it. Skin health follows from cellular energy and repair rather than from a delivery of protein the body was never short of.</p><p>Every dose is at the level used in the research it comes from, printed in full on the label. No proprietary blends, nothing you can’t check against what you already take.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
