        "faq1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "So collagen supplements do nothing at all?",
            "answer": "<p>That is not the claim. Some trials on hydrolysed collagen peptides report improvements in skin hydration and elasticity, and one proposed mechanism is that specific di- and tripeptides act as signals rather than as raw material. But much of that research is small and industry-funded, and it is a different mechanism from the one the marketing implies. What is not in dispute is the digestion: the collagen you swallow is broken down, and it is not delivered to your skin as collagen.</p>",
            "open_by_default": true
          }
        },
        "faq2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What happens to protein I don’t use?",
            "answer": "<p>There is no store for it. Surplus amino acids are deaminated in the liver - the nitrogen leaves as urea, and the carbon skeleton enters metabolism. Glucogenic amino acids, which is most of what collagen is made of, become glucose or glycogen. Ketogenic ones feed into acetyl-CoA and, in a calorie surplus, into fat.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I stop taking collagen?",
            "answer": "<p>That is between you and your own results. The point of this piece is narrower: if you are eating adequate protein you are unlikely to be short of the raw materials, so adding more of them is not where the gain is. The constraint is the energy and repair capacity to use what you already have.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How soon would I notice anything from MUTO?",
            "answer": "<p>There are no stimulants in it, so nothing in the first hour. Most people report the afternoon dip going in weeks three to four, and wearable data typically moves between day 30 and day 60. Skin is slow tissue - judge it across months, not weeks.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
